<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/9th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Field of Lies</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">833 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Field of Lies was the place near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colmar">Colmar</a> where, in 833 CE, the army of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> deserted to his rebellious sons. Louis was deposed and publicly humiliated, though he was restored the following year. The episode showed the deep divisions within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> inherited his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s empire in 814. Earlier Merovingian and Carolingian rulers had divided their kingdoms among their sons as private estates, but the idea of a single Christian empire protecting the Church made the unity of the realm a duty. Louis chose his eldest son Lothar as heir, crowned him co-emperor and set him over his brothers Pepin, who held Aquitaine, and Louis, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>.</p><p>After the death of his first wife, Louis married Judith, who bore him a son, Charles, in 823. His attempt to make Charles a co-heir alarmed the older sons, and dissatisfied nobles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> persuaded them that their father meant to destroy their inheritance. Lothar, Pepin and Louis formed a league and raised a large army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The brothers called on Pope Gregory IV, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> since 827, to reconcile them with their father, and rumours spread that he would excommunicate the emperor and his bishops if they resisted. In 833 the emperor, his three elder sons, their forces and the pope met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>. Gregory went between the camps, but his efforts at peace failed. Through bribes, threats and promises, the emperor's sworn supporters went over to his sons day by day, until he faced being overwhelmed if it came to battle. He met his sons on the field, reminded them of their promises, and at their demand left his camp for theirs, where he was held in a pavilion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The empire was divided three ways among the brothers. Gregory returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, Pepin to Aquitaine and Louis to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, while Charles was sent to Italy and Judith to a convent. Lothar kept his father under constant watch as he travelled, and kept for himself the gifts a Byzantine embassy had brought for the emperor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> performed public penance for his crimes against the empire and God. His son Louis was then compelled to make peace with him, and after a further shift of loyalties Lothar fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and the emperor was restored in 834. The reign is often taken as the start of the Carolingian decline.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Field of Lies” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Louis held the empire together through a reign of twenty-six years in which he was twice deposed by his own sons, most humiliatingly at the “<b>Field of Lies</b>” near Colmar in 833 CE, when his army deserted to the rebels en masse.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
